window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-06-2", "source": "2019年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of mutual understanding and respect in interpersonal relationships. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In our daily interactions, we inevitably encounter people whose habits, values and opinions differ from our\nown. In my view, mutual understanding and respect form the very foundation of any healthy interpersonal\nrelationship, and their importance can hardly be overstated.\nThe most compelling reason is that understanding and respect help us resolve conflicts and build lasting trust.\nWhen we make an effort to see things from another person's perspective, misunderstandings that might\notherwise escalate into quarrels can be smoothly settled. I once witnessed two roommates fall into a bitter\ndispute over daily routines; instead of blaming each other, they sat down, listened patiently, and reached a\ncompromise. That single conversation, rooted in mutual respect, transformed their tense coexistence into a\nwarm friendship.\nTherefore, it is safe to conclude that mutual understanding and respect are indispensable to harmonious\nrelationships. Once we learn to treat others with genuine empathy, we will find our connections far more\nrewarding. Let us cherish this principle in every relationship we build."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国幅员辽阔，人口众多，很多地方人们都说自己的方言。方言在发音上差别很大，词汇和语法差别较小。有些方言，特别是北方和南方的方言，差异很大，以致于说不同方言的人常常很难听懂彼此的讲话。方言被认为是当地文化的一个组成部分，但近年来能说方言的人数不断减少。为了鼓励人们更多说本地语言，一些地方政府已经采取措施，如在学校开设方言课，在广播和电视上播放方言节目，以期保存本地的文化遗产。", "sample": "China is a vast country with a large population, and in many places people speak their own dialects. Dialects differ greatly in pronunciation, but they differ little in vocabulary and grammar. Some dialects, especially those in the north and the south, differ so greatly that people who speak different dialects often find it difficult to understand each other. Dialects are considered a part of local culture, but in recent years the number of people who can speak them has been decreasing. To encourage people to speak local dialects more, some local governments have taken measures, such as offering dialect courses in schools and broadcasting dialect programs on radio and television, in the hope of preserving the local cultural heritage."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2019-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The dream of personalised flight is still vivid in the minds of many inventors, some developing cycle-powered craft, others  __26__  money into jetpacks(喷气飞行背包). However, the flying car has always remained the  __27__  symbol of personal transport freedom.\nSeveral companies around the world have produced  __28__  that can drive on roads and fly. Airbus has a futuristic modular(组件式的) concept involving a passenger capsule that can be  __29__  from the road-going chassis(底盘) and picked up by a helicopter-type machine.\nBut all these concepts are massively expensive, require safety certification standards for road and air, need  __30__  controls, involve complex folding wings and propellers, and have to be flown from air-strips. So they are likely to remain rich people's playthings rather than practical transport solutions for the masses.\n\"A car that takes off from some London street and lands in another  __31__  street is unlikely to happen,\" says Prof. Gray, a leading aeronautical engineer. \"Sky taxis are much more likely.\" But that won't stop inventors from dreaming up new ways to fly and trying to persuade investors to back their sometimes  __32__  schemes.\nCivilian aviation is being disrupted, not by the age-old desires for speed, romanticism and  __33__  , but by the pressing need to respond to a changing climate. New electric engines coupled with artificial intelligence and  __34__  systems will contribute to a more efficient, integrated transport system that is less polluting and less noisy. That may sound simple, but as Prof. Gray says, \"When I travel somewhere I like this notion that when I finish my journey I feel better than when I started it. That's completely at  __35__  with how I feel today.\" Now that would be progress.", "matchIntro": "Companies Are Working with Consumers to Reduce Waste", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "As consumers, we are very wasteful. Annually, the world generates 1.3 billion tons of solid waste. This is expected to go up to 2.2 billion by 2025. The developed countries are responsible for 44% of waste, and in the U.S. alone, the average person throws away their body weight in rubbish every month."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Conventional wisdom would seem to suggest that companies have no incentive to lengthen the life cycle of their products and reduce the revenue they would get from selling new goods. Yet, more and more businesses are thinking about how to reduce consumer waste. This is partly driven by the rising price of raw materials and metals. It is also partly due to both consumers and companies becoming more aware of the need to protect our environment."}, {"label": "C", "text": "When choosing what products to buy and which brands to buy from, more and more consumers are looking into sustainability. This is opposed to just price and performance they were concerned about in the past. In a survey of 54 of the world's leading brands, almost all of them reported that consumers are showing increasing care about sustainable lifestyles. At the same time, surveys on consumers in the U.S. and the U.K. show that they also care about minimizing energy use and reducing waste."}, {"label": "D", "text": "For the most part, consumers control what happens to a product. But some companies are realizing that placing the burden of recycling entirely on the consumer is not an effective strategy, especially when tossing something away seems like the easiest and most convenient option."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Some retailers and manufacturers in the clothing, footwear, and electronics industries have launched environmental programs. They want to make their customers interested in preserving their products and preventing things that still have value from going to the garbage dump. By offering services to help expand the longevity of their products, they're promising quality and durability to consumers, and receiving the reputational gains for being environmentally friendly."}, {"label": "F", "text": "For example, the Swedish jeans company Nudie Jeans offers free repair at twenty of their shops. Instead of discarding their old worn-out jeans, customers bring them in to be renewed. The company even provides mail-order repair kits and online videos, so that customers can learn how to fix a pair of jeans at home. Their philosophy is that extending the life of a pair of jeans is not only great for the environment, but allows the consumer to get more value out of their product. When customers do want to toss their pair, they can give them back to the store, which will repurpose and resell them. Another clothing company, Patagonia, a high-end outdoor clothing store, follows the same principle. It has partnered with DIY website iFixit to teach consumers how to repair their clothing, such as waterproof outerwear, at home. The company also offers a repair program for their customers for a modest fee. Currently, Patagonia repairs about 40,000 garments a year in their Reno, Nevada, service center. According to the company's CEO, Rose Marcario, this is about building a company that cares about the environment. At the same time, offering repair supports the perceived quality of its products."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In Brazil, the multinational corporation Adidas has been running a shoe-recycling program called \"Sustainable Footprint\" since 2012. Customers can bring shoes of any brand into an Adidas store to be shredded and turned into alternative fuels for energy creation instead of being burned as trash. They are used to fuel cement ovens. To motivate visitors to bring in more old shoes, Adidas Brazil promotes the program in stores by showing videos to educate customers, and it even offers a discount each time a customer brings in an old pair of shoes. This boosts the reputation and image of Adidas by making people more aware of the company's values."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Enormous opportunities also lie with e-waste. It is estimated that in 2014 the world produced some 42 million metric tons of e-waste (discarded electrical and electronic equipment and its parts) with North America and Europe accounting for 8 and 12 million metric tons respectively. The materials from e-waste include iron, copper, gold, silver, and aluminum materials that could be reused, resold, salvaged, or recycled. Together, the value of these metals is estimated to be about $52 billion. Electronics giants like Best Buy and Samsung have provided e-waste take-back programs over the past few years, which aim to refurbish(翻新) old electronic components and parts into new products."}, {"label": "I", "text": "For other companies interested in reducing waste, helping the environment, and providing the sustainable lifestyles that consumers seek, here are some first steps for building a relationship with customers that focuses on recycling and restoring value to products."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Find partners. If you are a manufacturer who relies on outside distributors, then retailers are the ideal partner for collecting old products. Power tool maker DeWalt partners with companies, such as Lowes and Napa Auto Parts, to collect old tools at their stores for recycling. The partnership benefits both sides by allowing unconventional partners (for example, two companies from two different industries) to work together on a specific aspect of the value chain, like, in this example, an engine firm with an accessory one."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Create incentives. Environmental conscientiousness isn't always enough to make customers recycle old goods. For instance, DeWalt discovered that many contractors were holding on to their old tools, even if they no longer worked, because they were expensive purchases and it was hard to justify bringing them in to recycle. By offering instant discounts worth as much as $100, DeWalt launched a trade-in program to encourage people to bring back tools. As a result, DeWalt now reuses those materials to create new products."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Start with a trial program, and expect to change the details as you go. Any take-back program will likely change over time, depending on what works for your customers and company goals. Maybe you see low customer participation at first, or conversely, so much success that the cost of recycling becomes too high. Best Buy, for instance, has been bearing the lion's share of e-waste volume since two of its largest competitors, Amazon and Wal-mart, do not have their own recycling programs. Since the launch of its program, Best Buy changed its policy to add a $25 fee for recycling old televisions in order to keep the program going."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Build a culture of collective values with customers. A stronger relationship between the retailer/producer and the consumer isn't just about financial incentives. By creating more awareness around your efforts to reduce waste, and by developing a culture of responsibility, repair, and reuse, you can build customer loyalty based on shared values and responsibilities."}, {"label": "N", "text": "These examples are just the tip of the iceberg, but they demonstrate how helping customers get more use of their materials can transform value chains and operations. Reducing waste by incorporating used materials into production can cut costs and decrease the price of procurement(采购): less to be procured from the outside and more to be re-utilized from the inside."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Companies play a big role in creating a circular economy, in which value is generating less from extracting new resources and more from getting better use out of the resources we already have—but they must also get customers engaged in the process."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Effective Friday, Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) has declared a strike against 11 video game publishers over games that went into production after Feb. 17, 2015. The companies include some of the heavyweights of the industry, like Electronic Arts Productions, Insomniac Games, Activision and Disney.\nThe strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor contract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014. Overall, the strike is an effort to provide more secondary compensation along with other concerns, such as transparency upon hiring talent and on-set(制作中) safety precautions.\nThe video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that the industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\nBut SAG-AFTRA says voice actors don't receive residuals(追加酬金) for their gaming work. Instead, they receive a fixed rate, which is typically about $825 for a standard four-hour vocal session. So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million subscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n\"It's a very small number of games that would trigger this secondary compensation issue,\" said voice actor Crispin Freeman, who's a member of the union's negotiating committee. \"This is an important aspect of what it means to be a freelance(从事自由职业的) performer, who isn't regularly employed every single day working on projects.\"\nAnother major complaint from the actors is the secrecy of the industry. \"I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show you're in, when you're hired,\" says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA negotiating committee.\n\"And yet that happens every day in the video game world,\" Farley told reporters during a press conference Friday. \"I was a main character in Fallout 4, a character by the name of Kellogg, and I never knew that I was doing vocal recording for that game throughout the year and a half.\"\nScott Witlin, the lawyer representing the video game companies, says voice actors \"represent less than one tenth of 1 percent of the work that goes into making a video game.\" So \"even though they're the top craftsmen in their field,\" Witlin says, \"if we pay them under a vastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to create far more problems for the video game companies.\"", "47": "Effective Friday, Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) has declared a strike against 11 video game publishers over games that went into production after Feb. 17, 2015. The companies include some of the heavyweights of the industry, like Electronic Arts Productions, Insomniac Games, Activision and Disney.\nThe strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor contract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014. Overall, the strike is an effort to provide more secondary compensation along with other concerns, such as transparency upon hiring talent and on-set(制作中) safety precautions.\nThe video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that the industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\nBut SAG-AFTRA says voice actors don't receive residuals(追加酬金) for their gaming work. Instead, they receive a fixed rate, which is typically about $825 for a standard four-hour vocal session. So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million subscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n\"It's a very small number of games that would trigger this secondary compensation issue,\" said voice actor Crispin Freeman, who's a member of the union's negotiating committee. \"This is an important aspect of what it means to be a freelance(从事自由职业的) performer, who isn't regularly employed every single day working on projects.\"\nAnother major complaint from the actors is the secrecy of the industry. \"I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show you're in, when you're hired,\" says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA negotiating committee.\n\"And yet that happens every day in the video game world,\" Farley told reporters during a press conference Friday. \"I was a main character in Fallout 4, a character by the name of Kellogg, and I never knew that I was doing vocal recording for that game throughout the year and a half.\"\nScott Witlin, the lawyer representing the video game companies, says voice actors \"represent less than one tenth of 1 percent of the work that goes into making a video game.\" So \"even though they're the top craftsmen in their field,\" Witlin says, \"if we pay them under a vastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to create far more problems for the video game companies.\"", "48": "Effective Friday, Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) has declared a strike against 11 video game publishers over games that went into production after Feb. 17, 2015. The companies include some of the heavyweights of the industry, like Electronic Arts Productions, Insomniac Games, Activision and Disney.\nThe strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor contract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014. Overall, the strike is an effort to provide more secondary compensation along with other concerns, such as transparency upon hiring talent and on-set(制作中) safety precautions.\nThe video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that the industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\nBut SAG-AFTRA says voice actors don't receive residuals(追加酬金) for their gaming work. Instead, they receive a fixed rate, which is typically about $825 for a standard four-hour vocal session. So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million subscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n\"It's a very small number of games that would trigger this secondary compensation issue,\" said voice actor Crispin Freeman, who's a member of the union's negotiating committee. \"This is an important aspect of what it means to be a freelance(从事自由职业的) performer, who isn't regularly employed every single day working on projects.\"\nAnother major complaint from the actors is the secrecy of the industry. \"I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show you're in, when you're hired,\" says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA negotiating committee.\n\"And yet that happens every day in the video game world,\" Farley told reporters during a press conference Friday. \"I was a main character in Fallout 4, a character by the name of Kellogg, and I never knew that I was doing vocal recording for that game throughout the year and a half.\"\nScott Witlin, the lawyer representing the video game companies, says voice actors \"represent less than one tenth of 1 percent of the work that goes into making a video game.\" So \"even though they're the top craftsmen in their field,\" Witlin says, \"if we pay them under a vastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to create far more problems for the video game companies.\"", "49": "Effective Friday, Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) has declared a strike against 11 video game publishers over games that went into production after Feb. 17, 2015. The companies include some of the heavyweights of the industry, like Electronic Arts Productions, Insomniac Games, Activision and Disney.\nThe strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor contract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014. Overall, the strike is an effort to provide more secondary compensation along with other concerns, such as transparency upon hiring talent and on-set(制作中) safety precautions.\nThe video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that the industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\nBut SAG-AFTRA says voice actors don't receive residuals(追加酬金) for their gaming work. Instead, they receive a fixed rate, which is typically about $825 for a standard four-hour vocal session. So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million subscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n\"It's a very small number of games that would trigger this secondary compensation issue,\" said voice actor Crispin Freeman, who's a member of the union's negotiating committee. \"This is an important aspect of what it means to be a freelance(从事自由职业的) performer, who isn't regularly employed every single day working on projects.\"\nAnother major complaint from the actors is the secrecy of the industry. \"I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show you're in, when you're hired,\" says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA negotiating committee.\n\"And yet that happens every day in the video game world,\" Farley told reporters during a press conference Friday. \"I was a main character in Fallout 4, a character by the name of Kellogg, and I never knew that I was doing vocal recording for that game throughout the year and a half.\"\nScott Witlin, the lawyer representing the video game companies, says voice actors \"represent less than one tenth of 1 percent of the work that goes into making a video game.\" So \"even though they're the top craftsmen in their field,\" Witlin says, \"if we pay them under a vastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to create far more problems for the video game companies.\"", "50": "Effective Friday, Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) has declared a strike against 11 video game publishers over games that went into production after Feb. 17, 2015. The companies include some of the heavyweights of the industry, like Electronic Arts Productions, Insomniac Games, Activision and Disney.\nThe strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor contract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014. Overall, the strike is an effort to provide more secondary compensation along with other concerns, such as transparency upon hiring talent and on-set(制作中) safety precautions.\nThe video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that the industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\nBut SAG-AFTRA says voice actors don't receive residuals(追加酬金) for their gaming work. Instead, they receive a fixed rate, which is typically about $825 for a standard four-hour vocal session. So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million subscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n\"It's a very small number of games that would trigger this secondary compensation issue,\" said voice actor Crispin Freeman, who's a member of the union's negotiating committee. \"This is an important aspect of what it means to be a freelance(从事自由职业的) performer, who isn't regularly employed every single day working on projects.\"\nAnother major complaint from the actors is the secrecy of the industry. \"I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show you're in, when you're hired,\" says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA negotiating committee.\n\"And yet that happens every day in the video game world,\" Farley told reporters during a press conference Friday. \"I was a main character in Fallout 4, a character by the name of Kellogg, and I never knew that I was doing vocal recording for that game throughout the year and a half.\"\nScott Witlin, the lawyer representing the video game companies, says voice actors \"represent less than one tenth of 1 percent of the work that goes into making a video game.\" So \"even though they're the top craftsmen in their field,\" Witlin says, \"if we pay them under a vastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to create far more problems for the video game companies.\"", "51": "Officials at the White House announced a new space policy focused on managing the increasing number of satellites that companies and governments are launching into space. Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\nThis policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of traffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new satellites can be launched. This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\nSpace, especially the space directly around our planet, is getting more crowded as more governments and companies launch satellites. One impetus for the policy is that companies are already starting to build massive constellations(星座), comprising hundreds or thousands of satellites with many moving parts among them. With so much stuff in space, and a limited area around our planet, the government wants to reduce the chances of a collision. Two or more satellites slamming into each other could create many more out-of-control bits that would pose even more hazards to the growing collection of satellites in space.\nAnd it's not like this hasn't happened before. In 2009 an old Russian craft slammed into a communications satellite, creating a cloud of hundreds of pieces of debris and putting other hardware at risk. Journalist Sarah Scoles reports that NASA currently tracks about 24,000 objects in space, and in 2016 the Air Force had to issue 3,995,874 warnings to satellite owners alerting them to a potential nearby threat from another satellite or bit of debris.\nThat's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a satellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris. It includes accounting for any piece of debris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or more. It might seem surprising to think about an item staying in space for that long, but the oldest satellite still in orbit—Vanguard 1—turned 60 in 2018.\nAgencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that would dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage. But for now, the U.S. government is more focused on preventing new debris from forming than taking the trash out of orbit.", "52": "Officials at the White House announced a new space policy focused on managing the increasing number of satellites that companies and governments are launching into space. Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\nThis policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of traffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new satellites can be launched. This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\nSpace, especially the space directly around our planet, is getting more crowded as more governments and companies launch satellites. One impetus for the policy is that companies are already starting to build massive constellations(星座), comprising hundreds or thousands of satellites with many moving parts among them. With so much stuff in space, and a limited area around our planet, the government wants to reduce the chances of a collision. Two or more satellites slamming into each other could create many more out-of-control bits that would pose even more hazards to the growing collection of satellites in space.\nAnd it's not like this hasn't happened before. In 2009 an old Russian craft slammed into a communications satellite, creating a cloud of hundreds of pieces of debris and putting other hardware at risk. Journalist Sarah Scoles reports that NASA currently tracks about 24,000 objects in space, and in 2016 the Air Force had to issue 3,995,874 warnings to satellite owners alerting them to a potential nearby threat from another satellite or bit of debris.\nThat's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a satellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris. It includes accounting for any piece of debris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or more. It might seem surprising to think about an item staying in space for that long, but the oldest satellite still in orbit—Vanguard 1—turned 60 in 2018.\nAgencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that would dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage. But for now, the U.S. government is more focused on preventing new debris from forming than taking the trash out of orbit.", "53": "Officials at the White House announced a new space policy focused on managing the increasing number of satellites that companies and governments are launching into space. Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\nThis policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of traffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new satellites can be launched. This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\nSpace, especially the space directly around our planet, is getting more crowded as more governments and companies launch satellites. One impetus for the policy is that companies are already starting to build massive constellations(星座), comprising hundreds or thousands of satellites with many moving parts among them. With so much stuff in space, and a limited area around our planet, the government wants to reduce the chances of a collision. Two or more satellites slamming into each other could create many more out-of-control bits that would pose even more hazards to the growing collection of satellites in space.\nAnd it's not like this hasn't happened before. In 2009 an old Russian craft slammed into a communications satellite, creating a cloud of hundreds of pieces of debris and putting other hardware at risk. Journalist Sarah Scoles reports that NASA currently tracks about 24,000 objects in space, and in 2016 the Air Force had to issue 3,995,874 warnings to satellite owners alerting them to a potential nearby threat from another satellite or bit of debris.\nThat's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a satellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris. It includes accounting for any piece of debris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or more. It might seem surprising to think about an item staying in space for that long, but the oldest satellite still in orbit—Vanguard 1—turned 60 in 2018.\nAgencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that would dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage. But for now, the U.S. government is more focused on preventing new debris from forming than taking the trash out of orbit.", "54": "Officials at the White House announced a new space policy focused on managing the increasing number of satellites that companies and governments are launching into space. Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\nThis policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of traffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new satellites can be launched. This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\nSpace, especially the space directly around our planet, is getting more crowded as more governments and companies launch satellites. One impetus for the policy is that companies are already starting to build massive constellations(星座), comprising hundreds or thousands of satellites with many moving parts among them. With so much stuff in space, and a limited area around our planet, the government wants to reduce the chances of a collision. Two or more satellites slamming into each other could create many more out-of-control bits that would pose even more hazards to the growing collection of satellites in space.\nAnd it's not like this hasn't happened before. In 2009 an old Russian craft slammed into a communications satellite, creating a cloud of hundreds of pieces of debris and putting other hardware at risk. Journalist Sarah Scoles reports that NASA currently tracks about 24,000 objects in space, and in 2016 the Air Force had to issue 3,995,874 warnings to satellite owners alerting them to a potential nearby threat from another satellite or bit of debris.\nThat's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a satellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris. It includes accounting for any piece of debris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or more. It might seem surprising to think about an item staying in space for that long, but the oldest satellite still in orbit—Vanguard 1—turned 60 in 2018.\nAgencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that would dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage. But for now, the U.S. government is more focused on preventing new debris from forming than taking the trash out of orbit.", "55": "Officials at the White House announced a new space policy focused on managing the increasing number of satellites that companies and governments are launching into space. Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\nThis policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of traffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new satellites can be launched. This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\nSpace, especially the space directly around our planet, is getting more crowded as more governments and companies launch satellites. One impetus for the policy is that companies are already starting to build massive constellations(星座), comprising hundreds or thousands of satellites with many moving parts among them. With so much stuff in space, and a limited area around our planet, the government wants to reduce the chances of a collision. Two or more satellites slamming into each other could create many more out-of-control bits that would pose even more hazards to the growing collection of satellites in space.\nAnd it's not like this hasn't happened before. In 2009 an old Russian craft slammed into a communications satellite, creating a cloud of hundreds of pieces of debris and putting other hardware at risk. Journalist Sarah Scoles reports that NASA currently tracks about 24,000 objects in space, and in 2016 the Air Force had to issue 3,995,874 warnings to satellite owners alerting them to a potential nearby threat from another satellite or bit of debris.\nThat's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a satellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris. It includes accounting for any piece of debris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or more. It might seem surprising to think about an item staying in space for that long, but the oldest satellite still in orbit—Vanguard 1—turned 60 in 2018.\nAgencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that would dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage. But for now, the U.S. government is more focused on preventing new debris from forming than taking the trash out of orbit."}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the man say he never really understood?", "options": ["A) Why Roman Holiday was more famous than Breakfast at Tiffany's.", "B) Why Audrey Hepburn had more female fans than male ones.", "C) Why the woman wanted to be like Audrey Hepburn.", "D) Why so many girls adored Audrey Hepburn."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Why so many girls adored Audrey Hepburn.\n【定位】I never really understood why so many girls were such big fans of her.\n我一直没真正弄明白，为什么那么多女孩是她的铁杆粉丝。\n【信号】说话人直陈\"没弄懂\"。男方原话 I never really understood why so many girls were such\nbig fans of her. 直接说出\"never really understood\"，这正是题干要找的\"他不理解的内\n容\"——说话人主动表态的句子就是答案句，不必再绕。\n【替换】so many girls were such big fans of her ↔ so many girls adored Audrey Hepburn\nbe big fans of = adored\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 把录音里出现过的 Roman Holiday 与 Breakfast at Tiffany's 两个片名拼成\"哪部更出\n名\"，可男方说的是没懂\"女孩为何爱她\"，根本没比较两片名气，属听到片名就想选的原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：I've seen the famous films, Roman Holiday, Breakfast at Tiffany's\nB) 偷换概念\nB 说\"为何女粉多于男粉\"，但录音只说 so many girls were such big fans，从未把女粉和\n男粉作数量比较；它偷偷把\"很多女孩喜欢\"扭成\"女比男多\"，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：so many girls were such big fans of her\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说\"女方为何想像 Audrey\"，可没懂的是男方，女方第1段说的是 I would give anything\nto be more like Audrey Hepburn（她自己的向往）。把\"女方的愿望\"安到\"男方不解的内\n容\"上，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：I would give anything to be more like Audrey Hepburn", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What prevented Audrey Hepburn from becoming a professional dancer?", "options": ["A) Her unique personality.", "B) Her physical condition.", "C) Her shift of interest to performing arts.", "D) Her family's suspension of financial aid."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Her physical condition.\n【定位】She suffered from inadequate nutrition during the war, and therefore, a career as a\nprofessional dancer would have been too demanding on her body.\n战时她营养不良，因此，职业舞者这一行对她的身体来说会太过吃力。\n【信号】因果词 therefore 引出落点。答案句以 and therefore 这个因果信号连接：前因是营养不\n良，后果是身体吃不消舞者职业。\"therefore\"后面正是题干要的\"妨碍原因\"，是听原因题最\n该锁定的信号词。\n【替换】too demanding on her body ↔ Her physical condition.\n身体吃不消=身体状况\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA\"独特个性\"在这段原因里完全没出现，录音讲的是营养与身体，不是性格阻碍了她跳舞，\n属原文未提及的捏造原因。\nC) 答非所问\nC 说\"兴趣转向表演艺术\"，录音确有 So she focused on acting instead，但那是\"身体原因\n→所以改行演戏\"的结果，不是阻止她跳舞的原因；考生易凭\"她后来去演戏\"的常识顺手选\n它，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：So she focused on acting instead.\nD) 未提及\nD\"家里中断了经济资助\"录音从未提及，妨碍她的是身体不是缺钱；它借\"家庭/经济\"这类听\n起来合理的常见理由凑数，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn about Audrey Hepburn in real life?", "options": ["A) She was not an outgoing person.", "B) She was modest and hardworking.", "C) She was easy-going on the whole.", "D) She was usually not very optimistic."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She was not an outgoing person.\n【定位】Her character in that film is very outgoing and charming, even though in real life, Audrey\nalways described herself as shy and quiet.\n她在那部电影里的角色非常外向、迷人，尽管现实中 Audrey 总把自己形容为腼腆而文静。\n【信号】让步词 even though 后才是真意。答案句用 even though in real life 这一让步信号把\"银\n幕形象\"与\"真实本人\"分开：even though 后面的 shy and quiet 才是题干 in real life 要的\n答案。让步/转折后才是落点，前半的 outgoing 是干扰。\n【替换】shy and quiet ↔ She was not an outgoing person.\nshy=not outgoing\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB\"谦虚勤奋\"中\"hardworking\"录音从未提及；虽然 shy 与\"谦虚\"略有交集易混，但\"勤奋\"完\n全没依据，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC\"总体随和\"对应的是录音里 very outgoing and charming——可那是\"电影角色\"(her\ncharacter in that film)的样子，不是真实的她；考生听到 outgoing 就想选，正中原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Her character in that film is very outgoing and charming\nD) 未提及\nD\"通常不太乐观\"录音里毫无对应，她被描述为 shy and quiet，与\"乐观与否\"无关；它利用\n与 A 同样的 not 句式制造混淆，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why did Audrey Hepburn devote much of her life to charity after her acting career?", "options": ["A) She was influenced by the roles she played in the films.", "B) Her parents taught her to sympathize with the needy.", "C) She learned to volunteer when she was a child.", "D) Her family benefited from other people's help."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Her family benefited from other people's help.\n【定位】Her family received international aid during the war when she was growing up. I think that\nleft a big impression on her.\n她成长期间，她的家庭在战时受到了国际援助。我想那给她留下了深刻印象。\n【信号】回忆受助 + left a big impression 点出触发。答案句先陈述往事 Her family received\ninternational aid during the war，再用 that left a big impression on her 点明\"正是这\n件事触动了她\"——这种\"往事+留下深刻印象\"的溯源句，就是动机题的答案信号。\n【替换】Her family received international aid during the war ↔\nHer family benefited from other people's help.\nreceived aid=benefited from help\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA\"受所演角色影响\"录音从未提及她的善心来自电影角色；动机原文明确归到\"家庭受助\"，\n属未提及。\nB) 偷换概念\nB说\"父母教她同情弱者\"，录音只说家庭\"受过援助\"，并未说父母\"教导\"她；它把\"family\"原\n词留着却换了动机机制，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：Her family received international aid during the war\nC) 原词陷阱\nC\"小时候学会做志愿\"借用了录音 when she was growing up（成长期间）这个童年时间，\n但原文说的是\"小时候家里受助\"，不是\"小时候学会志愿\"；听到\"小时候\"就想选，正中原词\n陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：when she was growing up", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is the man going to do?", "options": ["A) Give a presentation.", "B) Raise some questions.", "C) Start a new company.", "D) Attend a board meeting."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Give a presentation.\n【定位】M: Fine. I'm putting the finishing touches to it now.\n男：挺好的，我正在给它做最后的收尾。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。女士问 how is our presentation about the restructuring of the\ncompany coming along，男士紧接着应答 I'm putting the finishing touches to it now\n——一问一答里被问者亲口说在弄这份汇报，应答处即答案句。\n【替换】I'm putting the finishing touches to it now ↔ Give a presentation.\n代词it回指presentation\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里 we'll have to be prepared for questions 提到 questions，但那是说要准备应对听\n众提问，不是男士自己去提问；Raise some questions. 把这个词搬来错安在男士头上。\n借⽤原⽂：we'll have to be prepared for questions\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音反复出现 company、new structures 等词，Start a new company. 借这些词拼出开\n新公司，但全篇只在谈重组现有公司，从没说要创办新公司。\n借⽤原⽂：I'll outline the new company's structures\nD) 未提及\nAttend a board meeting. 听着像公司重组场合该有的事，但录音通篇只说 presentation，\n从未提 board meeting，属凭场景常识臆造、未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about the restructuring?", "options": ["A) It will cut production costs.", "B) It will raise productivity.", "C) No staff will be dismissed.", "D) No new staff will be hired."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） No staff will be dismissed.\n【定位】M: People are worried about their jobs, too. I think we need to stress that while there will be\nsome job changes, there won't be anyone getting dismissed. In fact, we're looking to take on\nmore staff.\n男：大家也在担心自己的饭碗。我觉得我们得强调，虽然会有一些岗位调整，但不会有任何\n人被裁掉。事实上，我们还打算多招些人。\n【信号】强调句won't加转折。男士用 stress 引出重点，再借 while there will be…but there won't\nbe anyone getting dismissed 的让步转折，把真意压在 won't 之后——不裁人才是他要\n传达的核心。\n【替换】there won't be anyone getting dismissed ↔ No staff will be dismissed.\nwon't anyone等于No staff\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt will cut production costs. 像是重组该有的好处，但录音从没提 production costs 或降\n成本，是凭常识臆造的未提及项。\nB) 未提及\nIt will raise productivity. 同样是想当然的重组好处，录音里 productivity 一词从未出现，\n属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\n录音末句 we're looking to take on more staff 明说要多招人，No new staff will be\nhired. 借走 staff 却把意思反成不招新人，极性正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：we're looking to take on more staff", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What will the man explain first?", "options": ["A) The timeline of restructuring.", "B) The reasons for restructuring.", "C) The communication channels.", "D) The company's new missions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The reasons for restructuring.\n【定位】M: I'll start with a brief review of the reasons for the change that we really need to make a\nclean break to restart growth. After that I'll outline the new company's structures and who's\ngoing where.\n男：我会先简要回顾一下这次变革的原因，也就是我们确实需要彻底切割、重启增长。之后\n我会讲新公司的架构以及谁去哪里。\n【信号】次序词start with。男士用 I'll start with a brief review of the reasons for the change\n的 start with 标出第一项，再用 After that 引出第二项，次序词清楚划定先讲原因。\n【替换】start with a brief review of the reasons for the change ↔ The reasons for restructuring.\nreasons for the change即重组原因\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe timeline of restructuring. 里的 timeline 录音确实提到，但那是 hand it over to you\nto discuss the timeline——时间线交给女士讲、且在后面，不是男士先讲的。\n借⽤原⽂：hand it over to you to discuss the timeline\nC) 原词陷阱\nchannels 在 we should let the staff know the channels of communication 出现过，但\n那是后面才聊到的内容、且非男士开篇先讲，The communication channels. 张冠李戴又\n错位。\n借⽤原⽂：the channels of communication\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说男士第二项讲 the new company's structures，The company's new missions. 把\nstructures 偷换成 missions，且 missions 全篇未提，既错序又改义。\n借⽤原⽂：I'll outline the new company's structures", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How can the staff learn more about the company's restructuring?", "options": ["A) By consulting their own department managers.", "B) By emailing questions to the man or the woman.", "C) By exploring various channels of communication.", "D) By visiting the company's own computer network."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By visiting the company's own computer network.\n【定位】W: Um, and we'll make the presentation and the questions available via the company's own\ncomputer network, right? / M: Yes, we'll make a page on the network where staff can\ndownload all the details.\n女：嗯，而且我们会把汇报和问答放到公司自己的电脑网络上，对吧？男：对，我们会在网\n络上做一个页面，员工可以在上面下载所有细节。\n【信号】提议加Yes确认。女士提议 make the presentation and the questions available via the\ncompany's own computer network，男士以 Yes, we'll make a page on the network\nwhere staff can download all the details 确认，提议加 Yes 的应答即敲定的途径。\n【替换】via the company's own computer network ↔ By visiting the company's own computer network.\nvia即By visiting\nmake a page on the network where staff can download all the details ↔\nBy visiting the company's own computer network.\n上网下载即访问网络\n【排除】A) 未提及\nBy consulting their own department managers. 听着合理，但录音从未让员工去问各自\n的部门经理，department managers 全篇未出现，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说 who they can contact or direct questions to 与 collect some frequently asked\nquestions 都提到 questions，By emailing questions to the man or the woman. 借走\nquestions 又拼上 email，但录音从没说用邮件发给男士或女士。\n借⽤原⽂：collect some frequently asked questions\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 let the staff know the channels of communication 里有 channels of\ncommunication，By exploring various channels of communication. 几乎照搬这个词\n组，但那是说要告知员工有哪些联络渠道，不是员工据以了解重组细节的途径，属答非所\n问。\n借⽤原⽂：the channels of communication", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is special about the Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky international airport?", "options": ["A) It helps passengers to take care of their pet animals.", "B) It has animals to help passengers carry their language.", "C) It uses therapy animals to soothe nervous passengers.", "D) It allows passengers to have animals travels with them."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It uses therapy animals to soothe nervous passengers.\n【定位】At the Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky International Airport, miniature horses deliver a\ncalming force two times a month.\n在辛辛那提/北肯塔基国际机场，小型马每月两次带来一股令人平静的力量。\n【信号】专有名词复现处即答案句定位锚。录音里 At the Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky\nInternational Airport 原样念出了题干中的机场名，这个专有名词的复现就是定位信号——\n紧跟其后的 miniature horses deliver a calming force 点明这家机场的特别之处是用小马\n带来\"平静的力量\"。\n【替换】deliver a calming force ↔ soothe nervous passengers\ncalming force = soothe，同义改写\nthe general anxiety that nervous passengers always feel ↔ nervous passengers\nnervous passengers 原词点明被安抚对象\n【排除】A) 未提及\ntake care of their pet animals 借用了录音反复出现的 animals、passengers，制造\"听到\n就想选\"的诱惑，但录音说的是机场\"提供\"动物来安抚旅客，从未提到帮旅客照看\"他们自己\n的宠物\"，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：interacting with travelers\nB) 原词陷阱\ncarry their language 听感上蹭了录音里 luggage（行李）这个词（选项 language 本身亦\n显错位），但录音提 luggage 是说动物习惯了 having luggage and people crowding\naround them\"行李和人群围着自己\"，并非动物帮旅客搬运，属盗用原词的答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：having luggage and people crowding around them\nD) 偷换概念\nallows passengers to have animals travels with them 用 animals、travel 拼出\"允许旅\n客带动物同行\"，迎合常识，但录音讲的是机场\"安排\"治疗动物去安抚旅客，主语和方向都不\n是\"旅客带自己的动物\"，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：therapy horses brought in from a local farm", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What are the trained animals probably capable of doing in an airport?", "options": ["A) Avoiding possible dangers.", "B) Finding their way around.", "C) Identifying drug smugglers.", "D) Looking after sick passengers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Finding their way around.\n【定位】These professional animals are probably better at finding their way in the airports than the\nmost frequent of travelers.\n这些专业动物在机场里认路的本事，大概比最常飞的旅客还要强。\n【信号】比较级 better at + 题干 probably 双重信号。答案句用比较级 better at finding their\nway in the airports than the most frequent of travelers 强调动物某项本领胜过常客，且\n题干的 probably 正对应原文的 probably，这种\"突出某能力\"的比较句就是描述动物能力的\n答案句信号。\n【替换】finding their way in the airports ↔ Finding their way around\nfinding their way 近乎原词的同义对应\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nAvoiding possible dangers 蹭了录音 The animals don't get startled\"动物不受惊\"的语\n境，听感像\"避开危险\"，但\"不受惊\"说的是性情稳、不被环境吓到，并非\"会避险\"，属过度\n引申。\n借⽤原⽂：The animals don't get startled\nC) 未提及\nIdentifying drug smugglers 利用大家对\"机场犬\"的常识印象（缉毒），极具迷惑性，但全\n篇讲的是治疗动物安抚旅客，从未提到识别毒品或走私者，属凭常识无中生有。\nD) 偷换概念\nLooking after sick passengers 借了录音高频词 passengers，但动物的作用是安抚紧张旅\n客、让人平静，录音没说照看\"生病的\"旅客，sick 属于凭空添加的限定，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：helps them to calm down before a flight", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do some passengers try to do?", "options": ["A) Schedule their flights around the animal visits.", "B) Photograph the therapy animals at the airport.", "C) Keep some animals for therapeutic purposes.", "D) Bring their animals on board their plane."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Schedule their flights around the animal visits.\n【定位】Some passengers enjoy the animals so much that they call the airport to schedule flights\naround their visits.\n有些旅客太喜欢这些动物了，以至于打电话给机场，把航班安排在动物来访的时间前后。\n【信号】Some passengers 主语复现 + so…that 结果句。答案句以 Some passengers 复现题干\n主语开头，又用 so much that 引出结果 they call the airport to schedule flights around\ntheir visits，主语对齐加结果句双重信号锁定这就是描述\"某些旅客所做之事\"的答案句。\n【替换】schedule flights around their visits ↔ Schedule their flights around the animal visits\nschedule flights around…visits 近原词对应\n【排除】B) 未提及\nPhotograph the therapy animals at the airport 利用\"旅客喜欢动物\"自然联想到拍照的常\n识，但录音说旅客喜欢动物的表现是\"打电话安排航班\"，从头到尾没提拍照，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nKeep some animals for therapeutic purposes 蹭了全篇的 therapy / therapeutic 与\nanimals，但治疗动物是机场从农场引进的，录音从没说旅客\"自己留养动物作治疗用\"，盗\n用原词后答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：therapy horses brought in from a local farm\nD) 偷换概念\nBring their animals on board their plane 借 animals 一词编出\"旅客带动物登机\"，但录\n音里旅客是被动物安抚、围着动物来访安排航班，并非把自己的动物带上飞机，方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：seeing animals makes them feel much better", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Where is the speaker standing?", "options": ["A) Beside a beautifully painted wall in Arles.", "B) Beside the gate of an ancient Roman city.", "C) At the site of an ancient Roman mansion.", "D) At the entrance to a reception hall in Rome."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） At the site of an ancient Roman mansion.\n【定位】Today I'm standing at the 2000-year-old Roman-era site. Here the brightly-colored scenes that\nonce decorated a mansion are being dug up.\n今天我正站在这处有两千年历史的罗马时代遗址上。这里,曾经装饰一座宅邸的鲜艳壁画正被\n发掘出来。\n【信号】现场指示语standing at。答案句的信号是说话人自报方位的现场指示语 I'm standing at\nthe 2000-year-old Roman-era site,紧接 scenes that once decorated a mansion 点明这\n处遗址正是一座宅邸所在,正是 where 的落点。\n【替换】Roman-era site ↔ the site of an ancient Roman mansion\nRoman-era等于ancient Roman\nscenes that once decorated a mansion ↔ mansion\n宅邸点题\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文后文确实提到这些发现出现在 Arles,本项 Beside a beautifully painted wall in Arles.\n借走 Arles 这个地名,但说话人自报的是站在遗址上(standing at the site),并非紧挨某面\nwall,把出现地点偷换成贴墙站立,属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：These scenes are turning up in the southern French city of Arles\nB) 偷换概念\n本项 Beside the gate of an ancient Roman city. 沿用了 ancient Roman 这一真实表述,却\n把说话人所站的 site(遗址)偷换成 gate of a city(城市的城门),原文从未提及任何城门,属盗用\n原词后偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：the 2000-year-old Roman-era site\nD) 张冠李戴\n本项 At the entrance to a reception hall in Rome. 把后文第二段才出现的 reception\nhall(接待厅)挪到此处,又把地点说成 in Rome;但说话人站的是法国南部 Arles 的遗址、且非\n站在接待厅入口,属张冠李戴式的原词拼贴。\n借⽤原⽂：Patches of paint still cling to the stone walls of the bedroom and reception hall", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do the thousands of fragments display when they are put back together?", "options": ["A) A number of different images.", "B) A number of mythological heroes.", "C) Various musical instruments.", "D) Paintings by famous French artists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A number of different images.\n【定位】These pieces have been put back together with great care and display a variety of images.\n这些碎片被非常细心地重新拼合起来,呈现出多种多样的图像。\n【信号】原词display直接对接。答案句信号是题干 put back together 与 display 在原文 put back\ntogether with great care and display a variety of images 几乎原样复现,顺着这一动作即\n锁定其宾语 a variety of images。\n【替换】a variety of images ↔ A number of different images\na variety of等于different\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文确实提到 a woman playing a stringed instrument 可能是 a character from\nmythology,本项 A number of mythological heroes. 借走 mythology 一词,却把一个举例\n放大成全部内容,且原文是 character(角色) 而非 heroes(英雄),属原词陷阱兼以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：possibly a character from mythology\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文举例提到一名女子 playing a stringed instrument,本项 Various musical\ninstruments. 借走 instrument,却把单个举例夸成 various musical instruments,展示的是\n图像而非各种乐器,属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：a woman playing a stringed instrument\nD) 答非所问\n本项 Paintings by famous French artists. 把绘画的来源说成法国名家,但原文第四段恰恰\n说工匠很可能来自意大利(came from Italy),且本题问展示的内容而非作者,属张冠李戴与答\n非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：experts believe the artists originally came from Italy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What makes experts think the paintings were done by artists from Italy?", "options": ["A) The originality and expertise shown.", "B) The worldly sophistication displayed.", "C) The stunning images vividly depicted.", "D) The impressive skills and costly dyes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The impressive skills and costly dyes.\n【定位】The paintings were done with such skill and with such expensive dyes that experts believe the\nartists originally came from Italy.\n这些壁画绘制得技艺如此高超、用料染料如此昂贵,以致专家相信这些艺术家原本来自意\n大利。\n【信号】such…that因果结构。答案句信号是 done with such skill and with such expensive\ndyes that experts believe… 里的 such…that 因果结构:that 前面的 such skill 与 such\nexpensive dyes 正是促成专家判断的两条依据。\n【替换】such skill ↔ The impressive skills\nsuch skill等于impressive skills\nsuch expensive dyes ↔ costly dyes\nexpensive等于costly\n【排除】A) 未提及\n本项 The originality and expertise shown. 中 expertise(专业技艺) 看似贴近原文的 skill,\n但原文给的两条依据是 skill 与 expensive dyes,并未提 originality(原创性),本项漏掉染料、\n又添了原文没有的原创性,要素配不齐。\n借⽤原⽂：done with such skill and with such expensive dyes\nB) 原词陷阱\nworldly sophistication 确在原文出现,但那是第五段说本地富人想借壁画炫耀见识,与专家判\n断画家来自意大利毫无关系,本项 The worldly sophistication displayed. 把别处原词搬来\n答非所问,属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：a wealthy local wanted to show off his worldly sophistication\nC) 原词陷阱\nstunning 一词原文出现在末段说后续拼合或带来更惊人的发现,本项 The stunning images\nvividly depicted. 借走 stunning 描述图像生动,却与专家判断产地的依据无关,属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：The paintings may yield even more stunning surprises", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the owner of the mansion?", "options": ["A) His artistic taste is superb.", "B) His identity remains unclear.", "C) He was a collector of antiques.", "D) He was a rich Italian merchant."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） His identity remains unclear.\n【定位】Perhaps a Roman official wanted Pompeii-like interior to remind him of home... Or maybe a\nwealthy local wanted to show off his worldly sophistication.\n也许是某位罗马官员想要一处庞贝风格的室内装饰来勾起他的思乡之情……又或许是某位富\n有的本地人想要炫耀自己见多识广。\n【信号】perhaps与or maybe的猜测并列。答案句信号是 Perhaps a Roman official 与 Or maybe\na wealthy local 这组 perhaps…or maybe 的猜测并列:原文给出两种互斥的可能、用 or 连\n接却都不下定论,正说明主人身份未定。\n【替换】Perhaps a Roman official ↔ His identity remains unclear\nperhaps或maybe等于不确定\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文夸的是壁画技艺高超(such skill),并未评价主人的艺术品味,本项 His artistic taste is\nsuperb. 由画好推主人品味好,是原文没说的过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：The paintings were done with such skill\nC) 未提及\n原文通篇在讲出土的古罗马壁画,但从未说主人是古董收藏家,本项 He was a collector of\nantiques. 是借古迹话题臆造的身份,原文无对应。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文说艺术家可能来自意大利(came from Italy)、又提这是一处贸易港,本项 He was a rich\nItalian merchant. 把 Italian 和 trading 拼成主人是富有的意大利商人,但 Italy 是说艺术家\n来源、且原文对主人是 official 还是 local 都未定,属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：experts believe the artists originally came from Italy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn about the research funding policies in the European Union?", "options": ["A) They encourage international cooperation.", "B) They lay stress on basic scientific research.", "C) They place great emphasis on empirical studies.", "D) They favour scientists from its member countries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They encourage international cooperation.\n【定位】In fact, the European Union is taking the lead. Spurred on by funding policies, half of\nEuropean research articles had international co-authors in 2007.\n事实上，欧盟正走在前列。在资助政策的推动下，2007 年有一半的欧洲研究论文拥有国际\n合著者。\n【信号】因果短语点出政策作用。答案信号是因果短语 Spurred on by funding policies，它点明正\n是资助政策推动了国际合著的激增，这句把政策与国际合作直接挂钩，是承载答案的句子。\n【替换】Spurred on by funding policies, half of European research articles had international co-authors ↔\nThey encourage international cooperation.\n推动合著等于鼓励合作\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 借了开篇 empirical research 与全篇科研话题制造熟悉感，但录音从未说政策侧重基础科\n研（basic research），讲的是推动国际合著，B 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：a new approach to empirical research\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 盗用录音原词 empirical（实证），把它包装成政策侧重实证研究；但 empirical 只出现\n在开场点题，与资助政策的取向无关，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：empirical research\nD) 与原文相反\nD 利用 European Union 让人联想到成员国，称政策偏袒本国成员的科学家；但录音说政\n策推动的是跨国合著，方向恰恰是对外合作，D 把范围从对外合作偷改成对内偏袒，与原文\n相反。\n借⽤原⽂：half of European research articles had international co-authors", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why do researchers today favor international collaboration?", "options": ["A) Many of them wish to win international recognition.", "B) They believe that more hands will make light work.", "C) They want to follow closely the international trend.", "D) Many of their projects have become complicated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Many of their projects have become complicated.\n【定位】the rising collaboration is partly out of necessity. This necessity comes with the rise of big\nscience. Many scientific endeavors have become more complicated. These new complications\nrequire the money and labor of many nations.\n合作的兴起部分出于必要。这种必要伴随大科学的兴起而来。许多科研项目已变得更为复\n杂。这些新的复杂性需要众多国家的资金和人力。\n【信号】out of necessity领出原因。答案信号是表原因的短语 out of necessity，它领出合作兴起\n的缘由，随后用 Many scientific endeavors have become more complicated 把这个必\n要性落到实处，是承载答案的句群。\n【替换】Many scientific endeavors have become more complicated ↔\nMany of their projects have become complicated.\n项目即科研活动近义替换\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 借了第八段 better visibility 谈论的国际认可这一好处，说成研究者想赢得国际认可的动\n机；但那是合作带来的回报，不是本题问的偏好原因，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：internationally co-authored papers have better visibility\nB) 未提及\nB 用谚语 more hands will make light work 表示人多力量大，听着像在说合作好处；但录\n音从未表达研究者因相信人多省力而合作，纯属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 拼接全篇高频的 international 与趋势话题，说研究者想紧跟国际潮流；但录音讲合作上\n升是出于必要与可能性增多，从未提跟风国际潮流这一动机，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：creating scientific coalitions across borders", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn about the field of mathematics?", "options": ["A) It requires mathematicians to work independently.", "B) It is faced with many unprecedented challenges.", "C) It lags behind other disciplines in collaboration.", "D) It calls for more research funding to catch up."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It lags behind other disciplines in collaboration.\n【定位】Mathematicians, by contrast, tend historically towards solitude. As a consequence, they lag\nbehind other disciplines.\n相比之下，数学家历来倾向于独自工作。其结果是，他们在合作上落后于其他学科。\n【信号】对比加结果双信号。答案信号是对比词 by contrast 加结果词 As a consequence：先以 by\ncontrast 把数学家与爱合作的物理学家对照开，再用 As a consequence 引出落后这一结\n论，结论句即答案句。\n【替换】they lag behind other disciplines ↔ It lags behind other disciplines in collaboration.\n落后表述近乎原句\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 借了 tend historically towards solitude 表达的独自工作，但录音说的是数学家历来倾\n向独处，而 A 说成数学要求数学家独立工作，把客观倾向偷换成强制要求，偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：Mathematicians, by contrast, tend historically towards solitude.\nB) 未提及\nB 说数学面临诸多空前挑战，听着与全篇科研主题相合，但录音谈数学只说它因独处而落\n后，从未说它面临空前挑战，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 盗用全篇高频的 funding，又借结尾 partnerships are rising there, too 暗示的追赶，拼\n成数学呼吁更多资助以迎头赶上；但录音从没说数学要更多经费，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：funding requirements that often require teamwork", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn from the Life magazine article?", "options": ["A) Scientists tried to send a balloon to Venus.", "B) Scientists discovered water on Venus.", "C) Scientists found Venus had atmosphere.", "D) Scientists observed Venus from a space vehicle."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Scientists discovered water on Venus.\n【定位】It told of how scientists rode a balloon to an altitude of 80,000 feet to make telescope\nobservations of Venus's atmosphere, and how their discovery of water raised hopes that there\ncould be living things there.\n文章讲述了科学家如何乘气球升到八万英尺高空、用望远镜观测金星的大气，以及他们发现\n水这件事如何让人们燃起 \"那里可能有生命\" 的希望。\n【信号】their discovery of water 是文章交代的核心成果。整句铺陈了气球、高度、望远镜、大气\n一连串细节，但真正落点是 their discovery of water raised hopes that there could be\nliving things there——\"发现水\" 才是这篇文章给出的关键结论，前面的气球和大气都是过\n程铺垫。\n【替换】their discovery of water ↔ discovered water on Venus\n名词化 discovery 还原为动词 discovered\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实说科学家 rode a balloon（乘气球），但乘气球只是观测的手段；Scientists tried\nto send a balloon to Venus. 把 \"乘气球升到高空观测\" 偷换成 \"试图把气球送往金星\"，气\n球根本没飞向金星，是听到 balloon 就抢选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：scientists rode a balloon to an altitude of 80,000 feet\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 make telescope observations of Venus's atmosphere 里 atmosphere 只是被观测\n的对象，文章并没说 \"发现金星有大气\"；Scientists found Venus had atmosphere. 把 \"观\n测大气\" 偷换成 \"发现存在大气\"，是借 atmosphere 这个原词答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：make telescope observations of Venus's atmosphere\nD) 偷换概念\n文章说的是 rode a balloon to an altitude of 80,000 feet to make telescope\nobservations——在地球大气层内乘气球用望远镜远观，而非 observed Venus from a\nspace vehicle（从太空飞行器上观测）。气球八万英尺远不等于进入太空，属于把手段升\n级、与原文不符。\n借⽤原⽂：rode a balloon to an altitude of 80,000 feet to make telescope observations", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What are scientists' findings about Venus?", "options": ["A) It resembles Earth in many aspects.", "B) It is the same as fiction has portrayed.", "C) It is a paradise of romance for alien life.", "D) It undergoes geological changes like Earth."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It resembles Earth in many aspects.\n【定位】The very first thing that scientists discovered with the mission to another planet was that\nVenus was not at all the Earthly paradise that fiction had portrayed. It is nearly identical to\nour own planet in bulk properties such as mass, density, and size.\n科学家在探测另一颗行星的任务中发现的头一件事，就是金星根本不是小说所描绘的那个\n\"地球般的天堂\"。它在质量、密度、大小等总体性质上几乎与我们的地球如出一辙。\n【信号】discovered + not at all 否定转折后才是真意。答案句以 The very first thing that\nscientists discovered 引出发现，紧跟 was not at all the Earthly paradise that fiction\nhad portrayed 一个强否定，先推翻浪漫想象；随后 nearly identical to our own planet\n才正面给出真实结论——否定之后的正面陈述才是科学发现的落点。\n【替换】nearly identical to our own planet in bulk properties such as mass, density, and size ↔\nresembles Earth in many aspects\nidentical=resembles，逐项列举概括成 many aspects\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n原文明明说金星 was not at all the Earthly paradise that fiction had portrayed——根本\n不像小说写的；It is the same as fiction has portrayed. 偏说 \"和小说写的一样\"，把原文\n的强否定 not at all 整个反了过来，是正话反说。\n借⽤原⽂：Venus was not at all the Earthly paradise that fiction had portrayed\nC) 与原文相反\n原文里 Earthly paradise 是被 \"not at all\" 否定掉的对象，第 2 段也只说金星 Venus\nquickly lost its romance（失去了浪漫色彩）；It is a paradise of romance for alien life.\n反把这份被否定的浪漫当成发现，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：not at all the Earthly paradise\nD) 过度推断\n原文后文确提到金星有 a tortured volcanic history（剧烈火山史）和活跃地表，但本题答\n案句强调的是金星 \"在质量、密度、大小上像地球\"，并未说它 \"像地球一样发生地质变化\"；\nIt undergoes geological changes like Earth. 把不同段落的信息硬拼、超出了本题答案\n句，是过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：suggested a tortured volcanic history", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What information did Russian and American space probes provide about Venus?", "options": ["A) It might have been hotter than it is today.", "B) It might have been a cozy habitat for life.", "C) It used to have more water than Earth.", "D) It used to be covered with rainforests."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It might have been a cozy habitat for life.\n【定位】Russian and American spacecraft also found hints that its ancient climate might have been\nwetter, cooler, and possibly even friendly to life.\n俄美航天器还发现了线索，表明金星远古时期的气候可能更湿润、更凉爽，甚至可能适合生\n命存在。\n【信号】found hints + ancient climate 是来源与时间的双信号。Russian and American\nspacecraft also found hints 直接对上题干来源，its ancient climate might have been\nwetter, cooler, and possibly even friendly to life 给出关于 \"远古气候\" 的发现——来源\n词 + 过去时间词双双命中，正是答案句。\n【替换】possibly even friendly to life ↔ a cozy habitat for life\nfriendly to life = cozy habitat for life\nmight have been wetter, cooler ↔ might have been\n保留 might have been 的推测口吻\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文清清楚楚说远古金星气候 wetter, cooler（更湿更凉），It might have been hotter\nthan it is today. 偏说 \"可能比今天更热\"，与原文 cooler 完全相反，是把凉听成热的反向陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：its ancient climate might have been wetter, cooler\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说金星当初的水 much more water than the tiny trace we find blowing in the\nthick air today——是 \"比今天金星上的痕量水多\"，比较对象是金星的现在，不是地球；It\nused to have more water than Earth. 把比较对象换成 Earth，借 water 这个原词答非所\n问。\n借⽤原⽂：much more water than the tiny trace we find blowing in the thick air today\nD) 未提及\n原文讲远古金星是 warm oceans, probably rich with organic molecules, splashing\naround rocky shores（温暖海洋拍打着岩岸），通篇没提雨林；It used to be covered\nwith rainforests. 凭 \"温暖湿润\" 自行脑补出 rainforests，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker mainly study?", "options": ["A) Causes of sleeplessness.", "B) Cross-cultural communication.", "C) Cultural psychology.", "D) Motivation and positive feelings."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） Cultural psychology.\n【定位】I specialize in Cultural Psychology, examining similarities and differences between East\nAsians and North Americans.\n我专攻文化心理学，研究东亚人与北美人之间的异同。\n【信号】开篇自报领域的 specialize in。I specialize in Cultural Psychology 用 specialize in（专\n攻于）直接报出研究领域，这种自我介绍式的点题句就是主旨题的答案信号，开头一响就该\n锁定。\n【替换】I specialize in Cultural Psychology ↔ Cultural psychology.\n领域名几乎原样对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n后文确实大谈 sleep，A 的 Causes of sleeplessness. 借\"睡眠\"勾人，但讲者研究的是文化\n如何塑造睡眠，从未把\"失眠成因\"当作主攻领域，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：We've also started to look at how culture shapes sleep.\nB) 偷换概念\nCross-cultural communication. 与原文 Cultural Psychology 都含\"cultural\"，但把落脚\n词偷换成 communication（沟通），而讲者明说的是 psychology（心理学），词面相\n近、内核已变。\n借⽤原⽂：I specialize in Cultural Psychology\nD) 答非所问\nMotivation and positive feelings. 对应第二段 self-enhancing motivations, how people\nhave positive feelings，那只是文化心理学之下的一个研究分支，被当成\"主要研究\"就是以\n偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：cultural differences in self-enhancing motivations, how people have positive feelings\ntowards not only themselves", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about North Americans?", "options": ["A) They attach great importance to sleep.", "B) They often have trouble falling asleep.", "C) They pay more attention to sleep efficiency.", "D) They generally sleep longer than East Asians."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They generally sleep longer than East Asians.\n【定位】East Asians on average sleep about an hour and a half less each night than North Americans\ndo. And it's not a more efficient sleep, not like they're compressing relatively more value out\nof their hours.\n东亚人平均每晚比北美人少睡约一个半小时。而且这并不是更高效的睡眠，并非他们在更短\n的时间里压榨出更多的睡眠价值。\n【信号】对比结构 less…than 锁定时长差。East Asians on average sleep about an hour and a\nhalf less each night than North Americans do 用 less…than 的横向对比直接给出两群人\n的睡眠时长关系，这种对比句正是细节题的答案信号。\n【替换】East Asians on average sleep about an hour and a half less each night than North Americans do ↔\nThey generally sleep longer than East Asians.\n比较方向对调的同值改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey attach great importance to sleep. 听着合理，但原文只比时长、从未说北美人\"重视\n睡眠\"，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nThey often have trouble falling asleep. 讲入睡困难，原文从未提及谁\"难入睡\"，把不存\n在的特点安到北美人身上。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 And it's not a more efficient sleep 明确否定\"东亚人睡得更高效\"，C 的 They pay\nmore attention to sleep efficiency. 盗用 efficiency 一词却拼成\"北美人重视效率\"，既偷\n换对象又违背原文否定。\n借⽤原⽂：And it's not a more efficient sleep, not like they're compressing relatively more value\nout of their hours", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "How did the speaker conduct the sleep study?", "options": ["A) By asking people to report their sleep habits.", "B) By observing people's sleep patterns in labs.", "C) By having people wear motion-detecting watches.", "D) By videotaping people's daily sleeping processes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By having people wear motion-detecting watches.\n【定位】Our experiment does not take place in a sleep lab. Instead, we lend people motion-detecting\nwatches, and they wear them for a week at a time.\n我们的实验并不在睡眠实验室里进行。相反，我们借给受试者动作监测手表，让他们每次连\n续佩戴一周。\n【信号】转折 Instead 后才是真做法。Our experiment does not take place in a sleep lab.\nInstead, we lend people motion-detecting watches 先否定\"实验室\"、再用 Instead 引\n出真实做法，转折/否定之后的内容才是答案落点。\n【替换】we lend people motion-detecting watches, and they wear them ↔\nBy having people wear motion-detecting watches.\n操作描述几乎原样对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nBy asking people to report their sleep habits. 的\"让人自报睡眠习惯\"原文从未提及，方\n法只有戴手表一种，属未提及。\nB) 与原文相反\nBy observing people's sleep patterns in labs. 盗用原文 sleep lab 一词，但讲者明说\ndoes not take place in a sleep lab，实验室恰恰是被否定的方式，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Our experiment does not take place in a sleep lab.\nD) 未提及\nBy videotaping people's daily sleeping processes. 的\"录像\"原文完全没出现，只是另一\n种听着合理的监测手段，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say about research on sleep?", "options": ["A) It has made remarkable progress in the past few decades.", "B) It has not yet explored the cross-cultural aspect of sleep.", "C) It has not yet produced anything conclusive.", "D) It has attached attention all over the world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has not yet explored the cross-cultural aspect of sleep.\n【定位】Sleep is something that has really been an unexplored topic cross-culturally.\n在跨文化层面上，睡眠一直是一个尚未被探索的课题。\n【信号】结尾概括句 unexplored…cross-culturally。末段 Sleep is something that has really\nbeen an unexplored topic cross-culturally 用 unexplored（未被探索）对睡眠研究下了\n整体判断，篇章结尾的这种概括句是现状题的答案信号。\n【替换】an unexplored topic cross-culturally ↔ It has not yet explored the cross-cultural aspect of sleep.\n形容词否定转主动否定的同值\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nIt has made remarkable progress in the past few decades. 说研究进展大，但原文反复\n讲 We are still in the exploratory stages 与 an unexplored topic，恰是\"刚起步、尚未\n探索\"，与选项相反。\n借⽤原⽂：We are still in the exploratory stages of this project\nC) 偷换概念\nIt has not yet produced anything conclusive. 同样含 not yet，但说的是\"没有定论\"，原\n文从未谈\"有无结论\"，只说\"未探索跨文化\"，把对象从\"探索范围\"偷换成\"研究结论\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Sleep is something that has really been an unexplored topic cross-culturally.\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt has attached attention all over the world. 的\"全球关注\"原文未提，疑似借讲者 I'm\nattracted to it 里的 attract/attention 类词面拼凑，实际原文只说讲者本人被吸引，并非全\n球关注。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm attracted to it because culture isn't something that only shapes the way our\nminds operate", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) pouring\n动词\n【词性槽】others ___ money 与 some developing 平行\n【依据】pour money into = 把钱砸进\nothers __(26)__ money into jetpacks 正是 pour money into sth 这一固定搭配——“向…\n砸钱/投入大量资金”，与“另一些发明者把钱投入喷气背包研发”语义完全吻合。\n【竞争词】L) repressing “压制金钱”讲不通\nrepress 意为“压制（情感/抗议）”，与 money into 这一搭配在语义上完全错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·others ___ money 与 s"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) ultimate\n形容词\n【词性槽】the___symbol：定冠词后、名词前作定语\n【依据】“终极象征”=个人交通自由的最高代表\nthe flying car has always remained the __(27)__ symbol of personal transport\nfreedom 配 ultimate 形成固定搭配 ultimate symbol（终极象征），与 has always\nremained 的“始终如一”语气也吻合。\n【竞争词】A) autonomous “自动驾驶”修饰 symbol 不通\nautonomous 形容车辆/系统能自主，不能形容“象征”这一抽象名词。\nC) dual “双重”留给后面 C 类两用控制\ndual symbol 不构成常见搭配，且 dual 的核心义“两用/双重”在后文 30 题 dual controls 处才\n用得上。\nE) imminent “即将到来”与 always remained 时间冲突\nimminent 指“迫在眉睫的”，与“一直以来都是…象征”这种长期状态矛盾。\nH) outrageous “离谱的”带贬义，与 freedom 不搭\noutrageous 含负面色彩，用来修饰“个人交通自由的象征”语义/感情色彩都不对。\nK) random “随机的”与“恒久象征”矛盾\nrandom 表“随意的”，与 has always remained 的稳定性完全相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the___symbol：定冠词后、名词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) prototypes\n名词\n【词性槽】have produced 后缺宾语，定语从句修饰\n【依据】“能开能飞的样品”=飞行汽车原型\nSeveral companies around the world have produced __(28)__ that can drive on roads\nand fly 描述的正是各家公司造出的“试制样品”，prototypes（原型机）与“能在路上开、又\n能飞”完美吻合。\n【竞争词】D) glamour 抽象“魅力”不能 drive/fly\nglamour 是抽象名词，不可数也无法成为 drive on roads and fly 的逻辑主语，搭配不成立。\nF) odds “概率/不和”与造车无关\nodds 常用于 the odds are/at odds with，意为“可能性/不一致”，无法被公司 produce，也不\n能开飞。\nG) opposites “对立面”语义跑偏\nopposites 表“相反事物”，与“能开能飞的实体”毫无关联。\nN) spectrum “范围/光谱”不可被开飞\nspectrum 抽象指“范围”，公司不能造出一个 spectrum 来开和飞。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·have produced 后缺宾语，定"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) detached\n动词\n【词性槽】be ___ from：被动结构作过去分词\n【依据】“与底盘分离”=detach from chassis\na passenger capsule that can be __(29)__ from the road-going chassis and picked up\nby a helicopter-type machine 描写“客舱可从底盘上拆下、由直升机吊起”——detach\nfrom 正是“从…拆下/分离”的标准搭配。\n【竞争词】M) segmented “被切分”≠“被拆下”\nsegmented 强调“分段/被切分成段”，描述把整体切成数块，而原文是“把舱体从底盘上整体分\n离”，方向不一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be ___ from：被动结构作过去分"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) dual\n形容词\n【词性槽】need ___ controls：名词前作定语\n【依据】“地空两用”需要“双重控制”\nrequire safety certification standards for road and air, need __(30)__ controls 上文反\n复强调“既要能开路、又要能上天”，dual controls（双重/两用控制系统）正契合“地空两套\n操控”这一现实需求。\n【竞争词】E) imminent “迫在眉睫的控制”不通\nimminent 修饰事件、危险等抽象概念，不与 controls 搭配。\nH) outrageous 贬义+ controls 不搭\noutrageous 表“离谱的”，与中性的“控制系统”语义不合。\nK) random “随机控制”与安全相悖\n上文谈到 safety certification standards，random controls 反而暗示无序，与安全语境矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·need ___ controls：名词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) random\n形容词\n【词性槽】another ___ street：名词前作定语\n【依据】“some street → another random street”对仗\ntakes off from some London street and lands in another __(31)__ street，some 与\nanother random 形成“随便起飞/随便降落”的对照，强调“在城市任意街道起降不现实”，\nrandom 完美承接。\n【竞争词】E) imminent “迫近的街道”不通\nimminent 不修饰地点，语义不搭。\nH) outrageous “离谱街道”偏离主旨\n原句重点不是街道夸张，而是“随意”，outrageous 偏离论点。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·another ___ street：名"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) outrageous\n形容词\n【词性槽】their sometimes ___ schemes：副词后修饰名词\n【依据】“有时离谱的方案”=贬义评价\nBut that won't stop inventors from dreaming up new ways to fly and trying to\npersuade investors to back their sometimes __(32)__ schemes 的转折语气里，\nsometimes 提示“偶尔离谱”，outrageous（骇人听闻的、离谱的）精确捕捉作者对发明者\n“天马行空”的微讽。\n【竞争词】E) imminent “迫在眉睫的方案”逻辑不通\nimminent 形容“即将发生”，但 sometimes（有时）与之冲突，且文意是“离谱”而非“即将\n发生”。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— imminent 易被误选为“即将出现”。their sometimes\n__(32)__ schemes，imminent 看似形容“即将到来的方案”有诱惑力；防坑要抓 sometimes\n这一频率副词——它要求形容词指“偶发性质”，而 imminent 强调“迫近”，逻辑冲突；语气\n也偏轻讽，应选 outrageous。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·their sometimes ___"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) glamour\n名词\n【词性槽】speed, romanticism and ___：三者并列名词\n【依据】“速度、浪漫、魅力”三位一体\nnot by the age-old desires for speed, romanticism and __(33)__ 列举民航历来追逐的感\n性诉求，glamour（魅力/光鲜）与 speed、romanticism 同属“感官与浪漫”范畴，三词同\n列十分自然。\n【竞争词】G) opposites “对立面”与“渴望”逻辑断裂\n对“对立面”的渴望不构成连贯语义，且 opposites 通常以复数表“对立事物”，与列举的抽象诉求\n不同层级。\nN) spectrum “光谱/范围”与浪漫诉求不同层级\nspectrum 偏中性技术词，对它的“古老渴望”不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·speed, romanticism a"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) autonomous\n形容词\n【词性槽】and ___ systems：名词前作定语\n【依据】“AI + 自主系统”是当下绿色民航主线\nNew electric engines coupled with artificial intelligence and __(34)__ systems will\ncontribute to a more efficient, integrated transport system——人工智能搭配“自主/自\n动驾驶系统”才能撑起更高效低碳的整合交通，autonomous systems 是这一语境下的固定\n表达。\n【竞争词】E) imminent “即将发生的系统”不通\nimminent 不修饰 system 这种实体技术，搭配不成立。\nH) outrageous 贬义不合“高效环保”语境\n全文此句正面评价新技术，outrageous 反义。\nK) random “随机系统”与“高效整合”相悖\n下文紧接 more efficient, integrated，random 反义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and ___ systems：名词前作"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) autonomous", "B) detached", "C) dual", "D) glamour", "E) imminent", "F) odds", "G) opposites", "H) outrageous", "I) pouring", "J) prototypes", "K) random", "L) repressing", "M) segmented", "N) spectrum", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) odds\n名词\n【词性槽】at ___ with：固定搭配 at odds with\n【依据】at odds with 固定搭配\nThat's completely at __(35)__ with how I feel today 表达“这与我今天的感受完全不一\n致”，at odds with 正是“与…不一致/相矛盾”的标准短语，由 completely 强化矛盾程度。\n【竞争词】G) opposites opposites 不进 at…with 模板\nopposites 表“对立面”，惯用 be opposites/opposite to，不与 at…with 搭。\nN) spectrum spectrum 与 at…with 不搭\nspectrum 常用 a spectrum of/across the spectrum，不能填入 at…with。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— opposites 偷换“对立”概念。completely at __(35)__ with\nhow I feel today，opposites 词性相符、词义也带“对立”味，最易诱导；防坑只看搭配模\n板：能填 at___with 的只有 odds，opposites 不进该框架。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·at ___ with：固定搭配 at"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Some companies believe that products' prolonged lifespan benefits both the environment and customers.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】prolonged lifespan + 双赢\n【定位】Their philosophy is that extending the life of a pair of jeans is not only great for the\nenvironment, but allows the consumer to get more value out of their product.\n他们的理念是，延长一条牛仔裤的寿命不仅有利于环境，还能让消费者从产品中获得更多\n价值。\n【改写】products' prolonged lifespan ↔ extending the life of a pair of jeans\n名词化展开成动词短语\nbenefits both the environment and customers ↔\nnot only great for the environment, but allows the consumer to get more value out of their\nproduct\nboth A and B 与 not only A but B 同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段说零售商和制造商 want to make their customers interested in preserving\ntheir products，强调的是「让消费者珍惜产品」与「品牌美誉度」，并没有把「对环\n境 + 对消费者」并列起来作为公司理念表述出来；F 段的 Their philosophy is that …\n才是题干「Some companies believe that」的同义改写。\nThey want to make their customers interested in preserving their products and preventing things\nthat still have value from going to the garbage dump", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "A survey shows shoppers today are getting more concerned about energy conservation and environmental protection when deciding what to buy.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】A survey + 节能环保购物\n【定位】At the same time, surveys on consumers in the U.S. and the U.K. show that they also care\nabout minimizing energy use and reducing waste.\n与此同时，针对美国和英国消费者的调查显示，他们也关心尽量减少能源使用和减少废\n弃物。\n【改写】A survey shows shoppers today are getting more concerned ↔\nsurveys on consumers in the U.S. and the U.K. show that they also care about\nsurvey/show/care about 原样复现\nenergy conservation ↔ minimizing energy use\n节约能源 = 最小化能源使用\nenvironmental protection ↔ reducing waste\n上位概念与下位行为\nwhen deciding what to buy ↔\nWhen choosing what products to buy and which brands to buy from\n买什么 = 购物决策语境", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Companies can build customer loyalty by creating a positive culture of environmental awareness.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】customer loyalty + culture of environmental awareness\n【定位】By creating more awareness around your efforts to reduce waste, and by developing a culture\nof responsibility, repair, and reuse, you can build customer loyalty based on shared values\nand responsibilities.\n通过让人们更关注你们减少废弃的努力，并培养一种以责任、修补和再利用为内核的文化，\n你可以基于共同的价值观与责任来建立客户忠诚度。\n【改写】build customer loyalty ↔ build customer loyalty\n短语原样复现\ncreating a positive culture of environmental awareness ↔\ncreating more awareness around your efforts to reduce waste, and by developing a culture of\nresponsibility, repair, and reuse\n上位概念 = 多个具体行为的概括\n【辨邻】 E E 段焦点是企业层面 receiving the reputational gains for being environmentally\nfriendly，并未提到培育顾客忠诚度；M 段才是把环保文化与 customer loyalty 直接\n挂钩的段落。\nreceiving the reputational gains for being environmentally friendly", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "When companies launch environmental programs, they will have their brand reputation enhanced.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】environmental programs → brand reputation enhanced\n【定位】By offering services to help expand the longevity of their products, they're promising\nquality and durability to consumers, and receiving the reputational gains for being\nenvironmentally friendly.\n通过提供帮助延长产品寿命的服务，他们在向消费者承诺品质与耐用性，同时也因环保形象\n获得了口碑收益。\n【改写】launch environmental programs ↔ launched environmental programs\n动词与名词短语原样复现\nthey will have their brand reputation enhanced ↔\nreceiving the reputational gains for being environmentally friendly\n「口碑被提升」= 「获得口碑收益」\n【辨邻】 G G 段的口碑提升 This boosts the reputation and image of Adidas 是「针对一家公\n司的具体结论」，而题干 companies 用复数泛指任何启动环保项目的企业，应当落到\n总论性的 E 段。\nThis boosts the reputation and image of Adidas by making people more aware of the\ncompany's values", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "One multinational company offers discounts to customers who bring in old footwear to be used as fuel.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】multinational + footwear + discounts + fuel\n【定位】Customers can bring shoes of any brand into an Adidas store to be shredded and turned into\nalternative fuels for energy creation instead of being burned as trash. They are used to fuel\ncement ovens. To motivate visitors to bring in more old shoes, Adidas Brazil promotes the\nprogram in stores by showing videos to educate customers, and it even offers a discount each\ntime a customer brings in an old pair of shoes.\n顾客可以把任意品牌的鞋子带到阿迪达斯门店，鞋子会被粉碎并转化为用于能源生产的替代\n燃料，而不是被当作垃圾烧掉。这些燃料用于水泥窑供能。为了鼓励顾客带来更多旧鞋，阿\n迪达斯巴西在门店通过视频做宣传教育顾客，并且每带来一双旧鞋都会给一次折扣。\n【改写】multinational company ↔ multinational corporation Adidas\ncompany 与 corporation 同义\noffers discounts to customers ↔\noffers a discount each time a customer brings in an old pair of shoes\n短语原样复现\nbring in old footwear ↔ shoes of any brand 与 brings in an old pair of shoes\nfootwear 上位概括 shoes\nto be used as fuel ↔ turned into alternative fuels for energy creation 与 fuel cement ovens\n「用作燃料」= 「转化为燃料并烧」\n【辨邻】 K K 段说 DeWalt launched a trade-in program to encourage people to bring back\ntools，对象是电动工具而非鞋，作用是回收材料制造新产品而非作燃料，跨国身份也\n未提，不能与 G 段的四元齐落相比。\nBy offering instant discounts worth as much as $100, DeWalt launched a trade-in program to\nencourage people to bring back tools", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Recycling used products can help manufacturers reduce production costs.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Recycling used products → reduce production costs\n【定位】Reducing waste by incorporating used materials into production can cut costs and decrease\nthe price of procurement(采购): less to be procured from the outside and more to be re-\nutilized from the inside.\n把使用过的材料纳入生产以减少浪费，可以降低成本并降低采购价格——外部采购变少、内\n部再利用变多。\n【改写】Recycling used products ↔ incorporating used materials into production\n「回收旧产品」= 「把使用过的材料并入生产」\nreduce production costs ↔ cut costs and decrease the price of procurement\n动词同义 + 名词上位概括\n【辨邻】 H H 段说金属材料 could be reused, resold, salvaged, or recycled，强调材料价值；\n但并未把「回收 → 制造商生产成本下降」这一因果链明说出来，因此不是题干的源\n头段。\nTogether, the value of these metals is estimated to be about $52 billion", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Electronic products contain valuable metals that could be recovered.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Electronic products + valuable metals\n【定位】The materials from e-waste include iron, copper, gold, silver, and aluminum materials that\ncould be reused, resold, salvaged, or recycled.\n电子垃圾里的材料包括铁、铜、金、银和铝——这些材料可以被再利用、再出售、回收或循\n环使用。\n【改写】Electronic products ↔ discarded electrical and electronic equipment and its parts\n上下位 + 形容词换名词\nvaluable metals ↔\niron, copper, gold, silver, and aluminum 与 the value of these metals is estimated to be about\n$52 billion\n形容词 valuable = 「估值 520 亿美元」的概括\ncould be recovered ↔ could be reused, resold, salvaged, or recycled\n上位动词 recovered = 多个具体动词清单", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It seems commonly believed that companies are not motivated to prolong their products' lifespan.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】commonly believed + not motivated to prolong lifespan\n【定位】Conventional wisdom would seem to suggest that companies have no incentive to lengthen\nthe life cycle of their products and reduce the revenue they would get from selling new goods.\n常识似乎会让人认为：企业没有动力去延长产品的生命周期，因为那会减少他们卖新产品的\n收入。\n【改写】It seems commonly believed ↔ Conventional wisdom would seem to suggest\n「commonly believed」与「Conventional wisdom」同义\nnot motivated to prolong their products' lifespan ↔\nhave no incentive to lengthen the life cycle of their products\n「motivated」= 「have incentive」、「prolong lifespan」= 「lengthen life cycle」\n【辨邻】 D D 段说 consumers control what happens to a product，但讨论的是「消费者控制\n产品命运、把回收负担全压消费者无效」，并未谈及企业是否被激励去延长产品寿命，\n与题干不对应。\nplacing the burden of recycling entirely on the consumer is not an effective strategy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It is advisable for companies to partner with each other in product recycling.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】advisable + partner with each other in recycling\n【定位】Find partners. If you are a manufacturer who relies on outside distributors, then retailers\nare the ideal partner for collecting old products. Power tool maker DeWalt partners\nwith companies, such as Lowes and Napa Auto Parts, to collect old tools at their stores\nfor recycling.\n寻找合作伙伴。如果你是依赖外部分销商的制造商，那么零售商是收集旧产品的理想伙伴。\n电动工具制造商德伟特与劳氏、纳帕汽配等公司合作，在它们的门店收集旧工具用于回收。\n【改写】It is advisable for companies to partner with each other ↔\nFind partners 与 DeWalt partners with companies, such as Lowes and Napa Auto Parts\n建议口吻 vs 段首祈使式建议\nin product recycling ↔ to collect old tools at their stores for recycling\n「in product recycling」= 「for recycling」\n【辨邻】 L L 段的小标题是 Start with a trial program, and expect to change the details as\nyou go，谈的是项目从小规模试点起步、随时调整，并未给出「公司之间合作」的建\n议，不与题干吻合。\nStart with a trial program, and expect to change the details as you go", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some businesses have begun to realize it may not be effective to let consumers take full responsibility for recycling.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】not effective + consumers take full responsibility\n【定位】But some companies are realizing that placing the burden of recycling entirely on the\nconsumer is not an effective strategy, especially when tossing something away seems like the\neasiest and most convenient option.\n但一些公司正在意识到：把回收的责任完全压到消费者身上并不是一个有效的策略，尤其是\n当扔掉东西看起来是最容易、最方便的做法时。\n【改写】Some businesses have begun to realize ↔ some companies are realizing\nbusinesses ↔ companies、begun to realize ↔ are realizing\nit may not be effective ↔ is not an effective strategy\n「not effective」原样复现\nto let consumers take full responsibility for recycling ↔\nplacing the burden of recycling entirely on the consumer\n「let X take full responsibility」= 「place the burden entirely on X」\n【辨邻】 B B 段虽然也含「companies + 否定」的元素，但是「常识认为企业没动力」，与题干\n的「企业自己意识到把责任压消费者无效」主语不同、论点也不同，因此不是源头段。\nConventional wisdom would seem to suggest that companies have no incentive to lengthen the life\ncycle of their products\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n用全球与美国的垃圾数据开篇，点明消费者很浪费这一问题的规模。\n信息指纹 全球垃圾数据\nB 指出常识认为企业没有动力延长产品寿命，但越来越多公司因原材料涨价和环保意识\n开始关注减少消费废弃。\n信息指纹 常识假设与逆转动因\nC 通过对 54 个全球品牌和英美消费者的调查，说明购物者越来越关注可持续生活方\n式、节能与减废。\n信息指纹 54 个品牌调查\nD 指出把回收负担全压在消费者身上并不有效，因为扔掉对消费者最省事。\n信息指纹 把责任全压消费者无效\nE\n服装、鞋类、电子等行业的零售商与制造商推出环保项目，通过延长产品寿命来兼顾\n环保、品质口碑和环保美誉度。\n信息指纹 环保项目带来美誉\nF\n用瑞典 Nudie Jeans 和户外品牌 Patagonia 两个例子，说明企业通过修补旧衣延长产\n品寿命，既环保又让消费者获得更多价值。\n信息指纹 Nudie Jeans 与 Patagonia 修补\nG 巴西阿迪达斯自 2012 年起开展 Sustainable Footprint 项目，回收各品牌旧鞋粉碎做\n水泥窑替代燃料，并以折扣鼓励顾客交旧鞋。\n信息指纹 Adidas 巴西旧鞋折扣换燃料\nH 聚焦电子垃圾：给出 2014 年全球与欧美的电子垃圾吨位、可回收金属的总价值，并\n提到 Best Buy、Samsung 的回收项目。\n信息指纹 电子垃圾里的贵金属\nI 承上启下，宣告下文要给「想减少废弃的公司」列出建立回收型客户关系的几条第\n一步。\n信息指纹 过渡：给出几条第一步\nJ 第一条建议：寻找合作伙伴；以 DeWalt 与 Lowes、Napa Auto Parts 合作回收旧电动\n工具为例，说明不同企业可联手做回收。\n信息指纹 Find partners 与 DeWalt 合作\nK 第二条建议：制造激励；以 DeWalt 用最高 100 美元的以旧换新折扣鼓励承包商交回\n旧工具为例。\n信息指纹 Create incentives 与以旧换新\nL 第三条建议：从试点项目开始、做好不断调整的准备；以 Best Buy 因竞争对手不做回\n收而调整电视回收收费为例。\n信息指纹 Start with a trial program\nM 第四条建议：与顾客共建集体价值观文化；通过营造责任、修补、再利用的文化来培\n育基于共同价值的客户忠诚度。\n信息指纹 Build a culture 与客户忠诚\nN\n总结性归纳：把使用过的材料纳入生产可以降低成本、降低采购价格，举例只是冰山\n一角。\n信息指纹 降低生产与采购成本\nO 结尾点题：企业要在循环经济中扮演重要角色，让消费者参与进来。\n信息指纹 循环经济收束", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why did SAG-AFTRA declare a strike against some video game publishers?", "options": ["A) The labor contract between them had been violated.", "B) Its appeal to renegotiate the contract had been rejected.", "C) It had been cheated repeatedly in the 19 months of talks.", "D) The negotiations between them had broken down."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why did SAG-AFTRA declare a strike against some video game publishers? 直接索取\n一个具体的事实性原因，不要求推断。\n【拆句】题干主干为 Why did SAG-AFTRA declare a strike against some video game\npublishers，一般疑问 + 介词短语限定罢工对象，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The strike comes in light of an unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations after the existing labor\ncontract known as the Interactive Media Agreement expired in late 2014.\n此次罢工源于一场为期 19 个月、未能成功的谈判——此前被称为《互动媒体协议》的现行\n劳动合同已于 2014 年末到期。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说合同 expired in late 2014，到期 ≠ 被违反，The labor contract between\nthem had been violated 属凭空增义。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文呈现的是 19 个月谈判失败，而非一方发起重谈被另一方拒绝；Its appeal to\nrenegotiate the contract had been rejected 把\"谈崩\"偷换为\"提议被拒\"。\nC) 无中生有\n原文只指出谈判 unsuccessful，并未指控对方 cheating；It had been cheated\nrepeatedly 既加重态度也添加事实。\nD) ✓ 正确\nan unsuccessful 19 months of negotiations ↔\nThe negotiations between them had broken down\n预判要求的\"谈了 19 个月没谈拢\"与 The negotiations between them had broken\ndown 字面对应——broken down 是 unsuccessful 的标准同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the video gaming industry?", "options": ["A) It has reaped huge profits in recent years.", "B) It has become more open and transparent.", "C) It has attracted many famous voice actors.", "D) It has invested a lot in its domestic market."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about the video gaming industry? 索取关于产业\n的事实描述。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about the video gaming industry：we learn 是\n套话，about 引出真正询问对象，无陷阱。\n【定位】The video gaming industry has ballooned in recent years. The Los Angeles Times reports that\nthe industry is in the midst of an intense increase in cash flow. In 2015, gaming produced\n$23.5 billion in domestic revenue.\n近几年游戏产业急速膨胀。《洛杉矶时报》报道称，该产业正处于现金流的迅猛增长之中。\n2015 年，游戏业在国内创造了 235 亿美元的收入。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ngaming produced $23.5 billion in domestic revenue ↔\nIt has reaped huge profits in recent years\nhas ballooned in recent years ↔ has reaped huge profits in recent years\n预判\"产业大赚\"与 reaped huge profits 完全同义，且 in recent years 与原文时间状语\n字字对应。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 the secrecy of the industry 明说产业\"保密\"，恰好与 more open and\ntransparent 相反。\nC) 无中生有\n原文出现 Crispin Freeman、Keythe Farley 等声优名，但只为传声其诉求，未说产\n业\"吸引了许多 famous 声优\"。\nD) 偷换概念\n$23.5 billion in domestic revenue 是产业从国内市场\"赚到的钱\"，invested a lot in\nits domestic market 把方向反过来变成\"向国内市场投资\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What are the voice actors demanding?", "options": ["A) More regular employment.", "B) A non-discriminatory contract.", "C) Extra pay based on sales revenues.", "D) A limit on the maximum work hours."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What are the voice actors demanding? 索取具体要求内容，是事实题。\n【拆句】现在进行时疑问句，结构简单。注意 demanding 是核心语义，不可漏读为一般疑问。\n【定位】So the voice actors are pushing for the idea of secondary compensation—a performance\nbonus every time a game sells 2 million copies or downloads, or reaches 2 million\nsubscribers, with a cap at 8 million.\n因此，声优们正在推动一种叫做\"二次报酬\"的方案——每当一款游戏销量、下载量或订阅数\n达到 200 万就发一次表演奖金，上限为 800 万。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nisn't regularly employed every single day 只是声优身份的事实描述，而非他们\"要\n求\"的内容；More regular employment 把背景偷换为诉求。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是 residuals/secondary compensation 与 secrecy，并未出现\ndiscrimination 概念；A non-discriminatory contract 是凭空贴标签。\nC) ✓ 正确\na performance bonus every time a game sells 2 million copies ↔\nExtra pay based on sales revenues\nsecondary compensation ↔ Extra pay\n预判\"按销量给的奖金\"与 Extra pay based on sales revenues 字面对应——extra pay\n= 奖金，based on sales revenues = 与销量挂钩。\nD) 偷换概念\nwith a cap at 8 million 的 8 million 指销量/下载/订阅触发奖金的累计上限，不是工\n时；a limit on the maximum work hours 用 cap 这一同形词钓鱼，但所限对象不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Keythe Farley say about voice actors?", "options": ["A) They are kept in the dark about many details of their job.", "B) They are discriminated against in the gaming industry.", "C) They are not paid on a regular basis.", "D) They are not employed full-time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does Keythe Farley say about voice actors? 直问其原话内容。\n【拆句】say about 结构常见：主语 Keythe Farley，宾语 voice actors，about 引出谈论对象，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】I can't imagine if there's any other acting job in the world where you don't know what show\nyou're in, when you're hired, says voice actor Keythe Farley, who chairs the SAG-AFTRA\nnegotiating committee.\nKeythe Farley 说：\"我无法想象世上还有哪种表演工作，是你被雇时连自己在哪部作品里都\n不知道。\"他是 SAG-AFTRA 谈判委员会主席。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nyou don't know what show you're in, when you're hired ↔\nThey are kept in the dark about many details of their job\nwhat show you're in ↔ many details of their job\nFarley 抱怨的核心是声优对工作内容的\"无知情\"，A 用 kept in the dark 是这一无知情\n的标准书面表达。\nB) 无中生有\nP6P7 的核心抱怨是 secrecy，未涉及 discriminated against；歧视议题在 Farley 这段\n并不存在。\nC) 张冠李戴\n\"不规律支薪/雇佣\"那条出自 P5 的 Crispin Freeman，与 Farley 无关；本题问的是\nFarley 的话。\nD) 张冠李戴\n全职/兼职话题属 P5 中 Freeman 对自由职业者身份的描述，Farley 在 P6P7 没说这\n件事。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the argument of lawyer Scott Witlin?", "options": ["A) Voice actors should have a pay raise if they prove to be top craftsmen.", "B) Changing the pay system would cause the industry more problems.", "C) Voice actors are mere craftsmen, not professional performers.", "D) Paying voice actors on an hourly basis is in line with the law."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the argument of lawyer Scott Witlin? 索取律师的论点。\n【拆句】What is the argument of X：of 引出观点持有人，主干为 what is the argument，结构平\n直，无陷阱。\n【定位】So even though they're the top craftsmen in their field, Witlin says, if we pay them under a\nvastly different system than the people who do the 99.9 percent of the work, that's going to\ncreate far more problems for the video game companies.\nWitlin 说：\"所以，尽管他们是这一领域的顶尖工匠，但如果我们按照与那 99.9% 的工作者\n截然不同的体系来支付他们，那将给游戏公司带来更多麻烦。\"\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\neven though they're the top craftsmen 是让步分句，承认声优是顶尖工匠只为引\n出\"即便如此也不能另立薪酬体系\"，而非 should have a pay raise。\nB) ✓ 正确\nif we pay them under a vastly different system ↔ Changing the pay system\nthat's going to create far more problems for the video game companies ↔\nwould cause the industry more problems\n预判\"另立薪酬体系会给公司添更多麻烦\"与 Changing the pay system would cause\nthe industry more problems 字面同义，B 把条件 + 结果两部分都覆盖到位。\nC) 无中生有\nthey're the top craftsmen in their field 明确承认 craftsmen 身份，但原文未把\ncraftsmen 与 professional performers 对立。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未出现 hourly basis 与 law 任何相关表述，in line with the law 完全无据。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the purpose of the new U.S. space policy?", "options": ["A) To lay out general guidelines for space exploration.", "B) To encourage companies to join in space programs.", "C) To make the best use of satellites in space.", "D) To improve traffic conditions in space."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the purpose of the new U.S. space policy? 是对原文已明示信息的直接提问，没\n有 infer／suggest 等推断信号。\n【拆句】标准 What is the purpose of X？ 句式，主语 the new U.S. space policy，无从句、无否\n定，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Space Policy Directive-3 lays out general guidelines for the United States to mitigate(缓解)\nthe effects of space debris and track and manage traffic in space.\n《第三号空间政策指令》为美国列出大致指南，以缓解太空碎片的影响并追踪、管理太空中\n的交通。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTo lay out general guidelines for space exploration. 把原文的 traffic in space 偷换\n成 space exploration，「探索」是与「交通管理」截然不同的活动。\nB) 无中生有\nTo encourage companies to join in space programs. 在原文找不到任何「鼓励公司\n加入」的表述，最接近的 companies are already starting to build massive\nconstellations 只说明现状，不是政策目的。\nC) 过度推断\nTo make the best use of satellites in space. 中 make the best use of 远超出原文\ntrack and manage traffic in space 的「追踪＋管理」语义，且话题对象从「交通」被\n换成「卫星」。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntrack and manage traffic in space ↔ improve traffic conditions in space\n预判要求落在「碎片」或「交通」两个语义里，四个选项中只有 To improve traffic\nconditions in space. 命中「交通」且与 track and manage traffic in space 等值，锁\n定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the Department of Commerce expected to do under the new policy?", "options": ["A) Reduce debris in space.", "B) Monitor satellite operations.", "C) Regulate the launching of new satellites.", "D) Update satellite communications technology."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the Department of Commerce expected to do under the new policy? 在原文\n里有明确职责描述。\n【拆句】What is X expected to do under Y？ 主干清晰，under 引导条件限定，无难点；结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】This policy sets the stage for the Department of Commerce to take over the management of\ntraffic in space. The department will make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio\nfrequencies that would interfere with existing satellites, and schedule when such new\nsatellites can be launched.\n这项政策为商务部接管太空交通管理铺平道路。该部门将确保新发射的卫星不会使用会与已\n有卫星互相干扰的无线电频率，并安排此类新卫星的发射时间。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nReduce debris in space. 出现在 to mitigate(缓解) the effects of space debris 和\npreventing new debris from forming 处，是政策总目标，不是 Department of\nCommerce 这一具体机构的职责。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 make sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies 只管发\n射前的频率分配，不是 Monitor satellite operations. 这种针对在轨运行的持续监控。\nC) ✓ 正确\nschedule when such new satellites can be launched ↔\nRegulate the launching of new satellites.\nmake sure that newly launched satellites don't use radio frequencies that would interfere with\nexisting satellites\n↔ Regulate the launching of new satellites.\n预判要求覆盖「频率＋时间」这两项发射环节的管控，Regulate the launching of new\nsatellites. 用一个上位动词同时统摄，恰是定位句的语义合集。\nD) 无中生有\nUpdate satellite communications technology. 中「更新通信技术」在原文无任何依\n据，最接近的 radio frequencies 仅指频率分配，并不是更新技术。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the U.S. government hope to do with the new space policy?", "options": ["A) Set international standards for the space flight industry.", "B) Monopolize space industry by developing a set of norms.", "C) Facilitate commercial space flights throughout the world.", "D) Promote international collaboration in space exploration."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the U.S. government hope to do with the new space policy? 在原文中有\nthe hope is that … 的明示表述。\n【拆句】What does X hope to do with Y？ 主干简单，with 表「借助／凭借」。无否定、无比\n较，无陷阱。\n【定位】This only applies to American space activities, but the hope is that it will help standardize a\nset of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world.\n这只适用于美国本土的太空活动，但希望此举有助于在新兴的全球商业航天产业中标准化一\n套规范。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nstandardize a set of norms in the dawning commercial spaceflight industry throughout the world\n↔ Set international standards for the space flight industry.\n预判要求「全球范围、统一标准、航天产业」三要素同时落实，Set international\nstandards for the space flight industry. 三点全中。\nB) 与原文相反\nMonopolize space industry by developing a set of norms. 把「标准化」扭曲为\nMonopolize 的「垄断」，原文 standardize a set of norms 是建立通用规范，与「独\n占」是反义。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文希望的是 standardize a set of norms，而不是 Facilitate commercial space\nflights throughout the world. 的「便利商业飞行」；后者是「统一规范」可能带来的\n间接效果，被偷换为政策的直接目的。\nD) 无中生有\nPromote international collaboration in space exploration. 把话题从 commercial\nspaceflight industry 偷换成 space exploration，并增加了 collaboration 这一原文未\n提的内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is a space vehicle launching entity required to do according to the current U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices?", "options": ["A) Give an estimate of how long its debris will stay in space.", "B) Account for the debris it has released into space at any time.", "C) Provide a detailed plan for managing the space debris it creates.", "D) Make a thorough analysis of any possible addition to space debris."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is a space vehicle launching entity required to do …? 问的是法规明文规定，原文\n有逐字依据。\n【拆句】主干 What is a space vehicle launching entity required to do？ 后接 according to …\n状语限定来源；动词 required 被动语态隐藏施事者，但不影响理解，无陷阱。\n【定位】That's why this new policy also includes directions to update the current U.S. Government\nOrbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices, which already require any entity that launches a\nsatellite or spacecraft to vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an\nunexpected failure or normal operations, will create more space debris.\n这就是为何这项新政策还包含更新现行《美国政府轨道碎片缓解标准做法》的指示。该做法\n已经要求任何发射卫星或航天器的实体严格分析其各种行为——无论是意外故障还是正常操\n作——产生更多太空碎片的可能性。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nGive an estimate of how long its debris will stay in space. 把原文 any piece of\ndebris they plan to release over 5mm that might stay in orbit for 25 years or\nmore 这一附加规定截取出来当主要义务；原文里停留时间只是判断要不要核算的门\n槛，发射方被要求的是分析创造碎片的可能性。\nB) 偷换概念\nAccount for the debris it has released into space at any time. 中现在完成时 has\nreleased 指向已发生的事实，而原文 vigorously analyze the likelihood that any of\ntheir actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will create more\nspace debris 是面向未来的可能性分析。\nC) 偷换概念\nProvide a detailed plan for managing the space debris it creates. 把「分析产生新\n碎片的可能性」扭曲为「为已产生的碎片做管理方案」，是 dispose of or capture\nspace debris 那种事后处置的语义，而非 vigorously analyze the likelihood。\nD) ✓ 正确\nvigorously analyze the likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal\noperations, will create more space debris\n↔ Make a thorough analysis of any possible addition to space debris.\nthe likelihood that any of their actions, from an unexpected failure or normal operations, will\ncreate more space debris\n↔ any possible addition to space debris\n预判要求三要素同时命中，Make a thorough analysis of any possible addition to\nspace debris. 一一对应——thorough ↔ vigorously，analysis ↔ analyze，possible\naddition ↔ the likelihood that any of their actions。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What are space agencies and companies aiming to do at present?", "options": ["A) Recycle used space vehicles before they turn into debris.", "B) Develop technology to address the space debris problem.", "C) Limit the amount of debris entering space.", "D) Cooperate closely to retrieve space debris."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What are space agencies and companies aiming to do at present? 问当下正在做的\n事，原文有明示。\n【拆句】主干 What are X aiming to do？ 加状语 at present 限定时间。aim to ＋ V 是固定结构，\n表「目标在于做某事」；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Agencies and companies throughout the world are working on developing technology that\nwould dispose of or capture space debris before it causes serious damage.\n全球各机构和公司正在致力于研发能在太空碎片造成严重损害之前对其进行处理或捕获的\n技术。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nRecycle used space vehicles before they turn into debris. 中 Recycle used space\nvehicles 与 before they turn into debris 在原文都找不到；原文 dispose of or\ncapture space debris 处理的是已经是碎片的对象，不是回收尚未成为碎片的航天器。\nB) ✓ 正确\ndeveloping technology that would dispose of or capture space debris ↔\nDevelop technology to address the space debris problem.\n预判要求「技术 ＋ 处理或捕获碎片」两要素同时命中，Develop technology to\naddress the space debris problem. 在动名词→不定式、上位动词替换两个层面均与原\n文等值。\nC) 张冠李戴\nLimit the amount of debris entering space. 对应的是末段次句 the U.S. government\nis more focused on preventing new debris from forming——这是政府的重心，主\n语是政府，不是题干所问的 Agencies and companies throughout the world。\nD) 无中生有\nCooperate closely to retrieve space debris. 中 Cooperate closely 在原文无任何依\n据，retrieve space debris 把 dispose of or capture space debris 这两个动词偷换为\n单一的 retrieve，并增加了合作意涵。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
